  "query_preview": "..Mostly dry conditions are expected across the forecast \nthrough the next week. \n\nToday...A weak, fast moving cut-off low is swinging through the \nregion. While we have been able to dry out over the past few days,\nthere is still enough moisture around for some isolated to \nscattered showers to develop by this afternoon. The latest high\nresolution models indicate initiation of showers not long after\nsunrise this morning in eastern Arizona. With cooler air aloft, \nthere will be enough instability to allow for some thunderstorms \nto develop. The best chances for any precipitation will be over \neastern Arizona and across the higher terrain, supported by\ncurrent water vapor satellite imagery. Rainfall amounts are \nexpected to remain light and generally around 0.10\" or less, with \na few locally higher amounts in heavier showers. As the low pulls \naway from the region, colder air will be drawn down to the surface\nand some snow may begin to mix in. Snow levels look to be mostly \nabove 8000', including areas like Buffalo Pass in the Chuskas, \nwith little to no accumulation expected.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...The departing low sinks to the south through \nthe weekend. The pressure gradient does tighten up a bit, so \nexpect some breezy conditions. Winds will be out of the north to \nnortheast around 5-15 mph in most areas with gusts to 20-35 mph \nalong and south/southwest of the higher terrain. \n\nMonday and onward...The pattern through the extended part of the \nforecast looks to remain mostly dry and mild. A weak trough may \ncross through southern Arizona on Tuesday, but with very limited \nmoisture it is unlikely we will see much more than an isolated \nshower or two develop, mainly towards the White Mountains. Looking\nfurther out toward the end of next week, most model clusters show\na deepening west coast trough that becomes a cut-off low. Current\ntracks keep the low sufficiently south to limit moisture returns \ninto the region. However, a shift northward could bring another \nround of showers to our area.",
